From 0e05d7b64565be62767cbad1b40a6f12e7abe4e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MasherDon <62326358+MasherDon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 02:48:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/91] moe-stream : stage the next layer's experts from the
 model's own prediction (A'), opt-in

DeepSeek V4.1: the graph of layer L computes A' for layer L+1 - the hyper-connection streams carried
past FFN L and attention L+1 as if both returned zero, collapsed with the mix attention L+1 computes
from them, then the router of L+1 (hash-routed layers route by token, exactly) - and hands it to the
remap of layer L as a second input, so it reaches the host with the same sync. The remap stages the
first LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_PRED_VRAM distinct predicted experts into VRAM slots of L+1, as the oracle's
staging does, and reads the first LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_PRED_RAM into the host tier. Both are 0 by
default, and then the graph is unchanged.

A wasted stage is now also counted when its slot is taken back before its call: a wrong guess has the
lowest route hotness of its layer, so the call's own misses usually evicted it before the count at the
end of remap saw it (~9k of ~26k wasted stages per run were missed).

DeepSeek V4.1-Flash Q2mix, 512 tokens, -ub 1, 17 I/O threads, tok/s (SiegeLadder runs.md 4.26): no
staging 9.76 / 9.86; A' top 6 into VRAM 9.77 / 9.84 (60% of the misses staged, 42% of the staged not
asked for); top 3 10.34; top 12 into the host tier 8.07. The oracle staging one call ahead without
host-tier anticipation - the ceiling of any one-layer predictor - 14.25 / 14.40. Every answer equals
the trace.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
